WEBVTT

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:59.480
Today, we are exploring the breathtaking Channel Islands, little British gems just off the coast of France.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:04.320
Now, I'm going to be staying here on Jersey and discovering the legacy left by world-renowned naturalist Gerald Durrell.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:09.400
And I'm heading over to Guernsey to unearth the fascinating story of the tomato industry that once thrived there.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:11.800
So I'll see you back here a bit later.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.240
Now, Ellie is not the only one on her travels.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:18.040
John will be setting sail to see what life is like on the tiny island of Sark.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.440
I mean, it sounds to be a very old-fashioned kind of community.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:27.840
Well, we're almost a step up from the Amish, except we've only got one husband!

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.800
Charlotte's back on the mainland doing a spot of weeding.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.760
This is black-grass.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:36.040
It doesn't look like much, does it?

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:41.640
But this resilient weed is rapidly becoming the scourge of British arable farms.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:44.240
I'll be finding out why.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.480
And Adam is meeting an unusual furry friend.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:53.240
Now, we all know what a pig looks like, and we all know what a sheep looks like.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.520
But have you ever seen one of these?

00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:00.080
I'll be finding out all about them.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:17.400
Clustering islands overflowing with exquisite coastlines, beautiful harbours and shaded lanes brimming with old-world charm.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:22.720
85 miles off the south coast of England sits the largest of the Channel Islands, Jersey.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:33.800
I'm on the north side of the island where one of our greatest naturalists spent much of his life - Gerald Durrell.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.880
Appropriately enough, Gerald's first word was "zoo".

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:42.920
His dream to own one came true in 1958 at the age of 33.

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:46.440
He made it his life's mission to save rare animals.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:58.400
Gerald Durrell died in 1995, but his extensive conservation work opened our eyes to man's impact on wildlife around the world.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:09.320
But Durrell cared just as much for the animals on his doorstep and, later on, I'm going to be meeting a native of Jersey whose cry is being heard over the north cliffs for the first time in over 100 years.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:15.520
For Durrell, self-sufficiency was hugely important, and it's still a significant philosophy of his wildlife park today.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.240
I'm heading to a very special farm he set up here.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:26.760
75% of the animals' food is grown on site, and I'm keen to see how they do it.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:28.240
But first, I've got a job to do.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:31.960
..Rubbish collection.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.720
I'll just chuck these up on here.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:35.360
I'm happy to help out, but I never thought I'd be emptying bins.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:41.280
MATT CHUCKLES 'At Durrell Wildlife Park, nothing is wasted.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:49.880
'Scrap office paper is the start of a recycling programme that 'saves around £6,000 a year.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.320
'That's cash that can help more endangered animals.'

00:03:53.840 --> 00:04:00.280
Well, this ash is a perfect example of Durrell's sustainable ethos.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:06.960
The waste paper from the office was shredded and given to the bears to lie on and to poo on.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:16.280
Their bedding was then dried, put into the boiler to heat the fruit bats, and now I'm delivering this ash to the farm so that it can go into the land and help produce food for all the other animals.

00:04:16.399 --> 00:04:25.440
'Dan Craven is busy harvesting an unusual crop that you wouldn't 'expect to find growing on a more traditional farm, 'prickly pears.'

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:27.560
Dan, which of your wonderful creatures will be eating this, then?

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.600
The tortoises love this.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:30.760
This is native to their environment.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:31.320
We're going to harvest a bit of this now, aren't we?

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.440
Be very careful.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:37.960
Some of these little thorns really mean it when they get into you.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:38.600
Hence I've got the gloves!

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:40.240
You're more experienced.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:42.640
No, I've just got thicker fingers!

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:43.360
OK.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:48.960
So, what's the technique when you're trying to harvest dinner for a tortoise?

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:52.480
So, if we can take this pad, maybe this pad here as well, that'll be a good start for us.

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:55.880
I have to cut left-handed now I've got the right-handed glove on.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:56.640
We're just going down at...

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:58.800
Yeah, cut just right down at the base...

00:04:58.080 --> 00:04:59.320
And they're so sort of succulent.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:00.600
That's why...

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:01.520
Oh, I've taken two off.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:02.600
Two for one!

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.120
Let's look in the wheelbarrow and see what you've harvested.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:13.800
Variety's the spice of life, they say, so we've got mizuna here, which is this sort of jaggedy leaf.

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:16.200
Sounds sort of jaggedy, the name mizuna.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:25.800
We've got mibuna, komatsuna, and these are the kind of varieties we're not able to get locally, we have to grow ourselves.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:29.040
And thinking back to when Durrell came here, he couldn't have chosen a better place.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:40.640
He couldn't have, and it shows how far ahead of his time he was, because we're able to obviously reduce our transport costs, and this polytunnel is able to sort of produce the kind of greens that I don't think anywhere else is doing, you know?

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:42.240
And certainly not in the conservation world in Europe.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:44.920
Well, you certainly know how to grow a cactus, I'll give you that!

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:45.400
Cheers!

00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:00.760
Well, I've experienced some growing and harvesting in my time, but this is quite different.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:12.280
Now, I'm down here, in an enclosure with the most endangered fruit bats in the world, it's feeding time, and, er, I feel like quite a target!

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:12.600
See what I mean?

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:20.880
'I'm hanging out with keeper Dominic Wormell 'and some Livingstone fruit bats.'

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:23.640
How often are you in here, Dom, shoving all this vegetation in?

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:27.040
They come in every day and they get this forage feed.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:28.720
They don't want to hurt you, do they?

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:29.080
There's a real misconception.

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:31.400
Bats, they're the most wonderful things.

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:31.760
Look at these guys.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:36.440
I mean, with their rounded ears, they're just cuddly, upside-down teddy bears.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:37.440
Yeah.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:44.120
And these are the farmers of the forest, you know, greater pollinators and seed dispersers than birds are.

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:45.960
When you're close up, like, they've got the most beautiful aura.

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:48.720
Yeah, they are just the most amazing animal.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:07:00.440
Do you know, this year would have been Gerald Durrell's 90th birthday, and it just seems a real shame that he's no longer around to see all of this.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:10.080
But from what I've experienced today on the farm and being so close to these animals, his legacy is still very much alive.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:20.880
Now, one thing farmers on Jersey don't have to worry about is a stubborn weed called black-grass but, as Charlotte's been finding out, it's becoming a huge problem for farmers on the British mainland.

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:29.600
'This is Mother Nature fighting back.

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:35.920
'You could say it's the modern equivalent of one of the seven 'plagues of Egypt.

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:38.480
'I've never seen a problem like this before in farming.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:43.080
'The whole management and even profitability of the farm 'is now controlled by this.'

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:50.280
This is a deceptively dull-looking plant.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:52.400
It looks harmless, doesn't it?

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:56.280
But don't be fooled.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:58.560
This is bringing arable businesses to their knees.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:03.480
It's called black-grass, and it spreads like wildfire.

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:07.520
'Not that pulling at it will do any good.

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:10.480
'This field is riddled with the stuff.

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:18.040
'It won't be long before purple seed heads appear, 'giving the field the dark appearance that gives this weed its name.

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:27.040
'Black-grass IS native to the UK, but since the 1980s 'new, tougher varieties have spread across the country.

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:32.360
'Farmers try to control it using herbicides.'

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:37.000
The problem is, black-grass is increasingly resistant to those chemicals.

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:42.880
'This weed refuses to die, 'and farmers are running out of ways to fight it.

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:53.680
'Tom Bradshaw farms nearly 4,000 acres in Essex, all green and lush, 'except this field, burnt, bare and barren.'

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:56.760
What happened here?

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:06.840
What you can see in this field is a pretty tragic result of some of the pressures we're currently facing, so we've decided to spray this whole crop off.

00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:07.320
But that's insane.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:07.800
You've destroyed your crop, you've destroyed any hopes of a profit.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:12.720
For this year, we have written off making any money out of this field.

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:16.720
We've got a big loss on it, because we've already spent money on it.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:21.960
But when you look down into the bottom of the crop, you can see there's a lot of black-grass plants just in the bottom of the crop here.

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:23.240
Yeah.

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:35.320
And if we hadn't have taken the decision to spray it off, the seed return from those plants was probably going to mean we couldn't crop this field next year or the year after, and so it's a short-term pain for hopefully a long-term gain.

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:40.000
This is a huge field of nothing now.

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:46.320
No, and this was top-quality milling wheat we were hoping to produce here.

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:53.560
It was all going to go into the bread-wheat market, and we'd have probably produced about 160,000 loaves of bread from this one field.

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:58.760
That's bread which is not going to be on supermarket shelves any more.

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:04.600
'In effect, black-grass strangles wheat, 'choking it before it has a chance to grow.

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:08.240
'And because herbicides won't kill it, Tom had no choice 'but to sacrifice his crop.

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:09.440
'He's not the only one suffering.'

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:18.520
Herbicide-resistant black-grass has now been found on 16,000 farms in 34 counties, and it's spreading.

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:22.600
So how did it develop this clever resistance to chemicals which used to kill it?

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:27.160
'Well, one man who knows the answer is Dr Stephen Moss.

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:29.440
'He's come to take a look at the problem on Tom's farm.'

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:33.720
I understand you know a bit about black-grass.

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:40.080
Well, I've worked on it for over 40 years, so I like to think I've done more work on it than anyone else in the universe!

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:42.800
CHARLOTTE LAUGHS That may be a bit of a boast, but it's probably true.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:47.160
So, you have some hi-tech equipment to help in the process(!)

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:48.920
What do you do with this?

00:10:48.080 --> 00:11:04.920
Well, this is a quadrat, and one puts it over black-grass, and you go round the field at random to count the numbers of black-grass heads so you can get an idea of the density and then how much effect that will then have on yield.

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:11.600
I mean, this is a fairly high infestation, but you can get over 1,000 a square metre, and that's nothing like that here.

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:17.400
So, how come this has developed to be resistant, then, to the herbicides?

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:24.280
Well, black-grass is a very adaptable weed, so it's evolved resistance through selection, the rare individuals which have natural resistance.

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:33.000
And they build up, they shed seeds, and the population builds up over a period of years until you get perhaps a whole population that's resistant.

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:37.040
And actually, as we walk round this field, once you start seeing it, you can see it everywhere.

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:37.800
It's not the only thing, though, is it, that's resistant?

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:38.920
What other things do we need to worry about?

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:56.760
We have rye-grass, wild oats, which are resistant in the UK, we have poppies, chickweed and indeed mayweed, but black-grass is certainly the worst case, and it's the worst resistant weed in Western Europe, and it's one of the worst in the world.

00:11:56.760 --> 00:12:04.240
'But there are those who believe farmers have brought 'this on themselves by being too dependent on pesticides.

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:11.400
'Stephen Fell is the chairman of Leaf, 'an organisation which promotes sustainable farming.

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:12.240
'He doesn't mince his words.'

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:15.320
I think we've forgotten how to farm properly, Charlotte.

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:16.760
We've got lazy.

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:28.520
We're growing continuous cereals, continuous combinable crops, and we've got away from good rotational practice, and we've relied too much on the fertiliser bag and the chemical can.

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:31.560
It seems a bit unfair to blame lazy farming, because that's with the benefit of hindsight, isn't it?

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:36.040
As far as farmers have been concerned, they're just doing the sensible thing.

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:56.080
Well, let's look back to the '80s and early '90s, when we had wonderful chemical technology and learnt how to use fertilisers, so our yields went up every year, we clapped ourselves on the back and said, "We're great at this," and then all of a sudden it came to a halt and the yields plateau'd and suddenly black-grass started to appear.

00:12:56.120 --> 00:13:08.040
'Stephen claims intensive agriculture has overworked the soil, 'weakening its ability to nourish strong crops, 'and that's given the weeds a chance to take over.

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:15.680
'Farmers argue they've always used the best tools at their disposal 'and for a long time herbicides worked.

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:19.600
'However we got here, the industry faces a real challenge.'

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:28.000
Everyone agrees something must be done about black-grass, and fast.

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:29.640
So, what, exactly?

00:13:28.840 --> 00:13:31.040
Well, I'll try and answer that question later.

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:50.760
'Guernsey, lying to the northeast of Jersey, 'just 24 square miles of patchwork fields and idyllic coastline.'

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:56.200
70 years ago this month, Guernsey was liberated from Nazi occupation.

00:13:55.640 --> 00:14:01.920
For five long years, the island was held to ransom by the demands of its captors.

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:14.360
'We're all familiar with the stories of the devastation 'and tragedy of the Second World War, but the effect of the 'occupation on the farming community of Guernsey isn't as well known.

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:22.920
'The Guernsey tomato had been an important 'source of income on the island for nearly 100 years 'and an established part of the island's culture.

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:29.320
'In the 1930s, roughly 1 in 15 of the population were growers.'

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:35.520
But soon, this hard-working community found themselves on the front line of the Nazi invasion.

00:14:36.200 --> 00:14:44.800
'Molly Bihet, who lived near the harbour of St Peter Port, 'witnessed the German invasion of the island.

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:45.920
'She was just nine years old.'

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:53.920
Before the raid, it was just a nice place to be, because there were so many...

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:55.680
People would meet there.

00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:59.560
There was a lot of exporting, especially the tomatoes.

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:03.240
My grandfather used to go every day to have a chat.

00:15:02.440 --> 00:15:05.280
It was a very busy place.

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:13.200
'The summer evening of the 28th of June 1940 was no exception.'

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:16.720
What were you doing on the day of the raid?

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:29.000
We'd been to a shop buying vegetables, and I had this...cucumber, and we heard these planes coming, and we thought they were British and we started waving.

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:39.840
Of course, they came down very low, and then all of a sudden we could see the German signs, we could see the machine-gunning, the planes came so low over the harbour.

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:40.000
Where did they fly from?

00:15:39.320 --> 00:15:42.080
They came from the north.

00:15:41.240 --> 00:15:43.680
We were terrified.

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:53.600
'Molly and her mum hid in a neighbour's cellar while her 'grandfather and uncle, who were at the harbour, hid under the jetty.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:58.080
'But for the dozens of fruit growers queuing in the port, there was 'nowhere safe to run.'

00:15:57.800 --> 00:16:01.040
What happened to the tomato farmers that were there?

00:16:00.080 --> 00:16:05.520
The lorries were all burning, because they were full of tomatoes for exporting.

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:12.920
The drivers had got underneath the lorries, thinking they were protected from the machine-gunning.

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:13.360
Of course, they weren't.

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:19.440
'34 civilians died from the attack, and many more were injured.

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:24.840
'But the impact of the war on the island's horticultural 'industry had only just begun.'

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:34.040
'Suzanne Brewer is a third-generation tomato grower.

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:36.760
'Many of her relatives lived and worked through the occupation.'

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:40.480
How did life change for them when the occupation began?

00:16:40.960 --> 00:16:49.080
Well, after the bombing down at the harbour, exports of tomatoes had to stop.

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:54.960
'The managers of the vineries had to sort of step aside 'and be told what to grow and when to grow it.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:17:00.000
'Suzanne still has her family's diaries, 'which tell of the harsh reality of life at the time.'

00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:02.280
It's incredible to have these.

00:17:02.280 --> 00:17:05.119
Can we have a look at some of their entries to see what went on?

00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:09.319
This is Uncle Arthur's diary from the beginning of the occupation.

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:20.079
"Tomatoes abound, and every cow and person is confronted with "the colossal task of eating 400,000 baskets of ripe fruit."

00:17:19.599 --> 00:17:22.119
'But the surplus didn't last.

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:31.000
'By 1944, the islanders were starving and 'so too were the Nazi soldiers, who went to extreme lengths to survive.'

00:17:30.160 --> 00:17:38.160
The tone in Uncle Arthur's diaries from 1940 to 1945 changes quite dramatically.

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:40.720
"March 31st 1944.

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:46.280
"I have had four robberies within 24 hours "and now they've been after my cow.

00:17:45.440 --> 00:17:51.160
"No words of mine can describe the feeling of insecurity "about everything everywhere."

00:17:51.040 --> 00:17:56.760
There was just an enormous amount of thieving going on, and sometimes it got quite violent.

00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:59.560
There's another entry here.

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:03.400
Maybe, Ellie, you'd like to read it.

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:16.600
"Then Mr Mahy told me a greenhouse robber, a soldier, "had shot dead a German Gendarmerie man who caught him "robbing broccoli or potatoes last night."

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:18.840
So killing each other in their desperation.

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:29.400
'70 years on, and tomato farming under Nazi occupation is 'a memory held by a diminishing few.

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:34.880
'After a post-war boom, the industry fell into decline.'

00:18:34.240 --> 00:18:41.160
In fact, there are now only a few commercial growers left on the island.

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:49.600
But there's a new project that's been set up with the aim of keeping that knowledge of the industry alive.

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:57.280
'One of the founders of the project is Jock Pettitt.'

00:18:56.680 --> 00:18:59.040
What's this project all about?

00:18:59.480 --> 00:19:13.400
Edible Guernsey is about bringing the infrastructure and expertise that was left over from the decline of the growing industries here and using them to teach people about food education and provide food security.

00:19:12.440 --> 00:19:14.040
And starting with children?

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:18.040
Children getting involved is fantastic, because food education obviously starts young.

00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:20.640
We've got people like Nigel as a mentor.

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:25.800
There's a few others who have significant expertise in growing on the island.

00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:31.680
But we import most of our food, so we saw an opportunity to try and produce food locally.

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:40.280
'The tomato industry may be almost gone, but the knowledge gained by the 'growers of Guernsey in good times and bad will now hopefully live on.'

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:58.320
'Just a few miles east of Guernsey sits a place which has been 'described as "the jewel in the crown of the Channel Islands".

00:19:57.400 --> 00:20:03.880
'It's not hard to see why 'when you take in Sark's beautiful coastline and patchwork of fields.

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:10.520
'But what's life like on this small island for the 600 or so 'people who call it home?'

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:16.160
You can't just fly in, unless you know somebody with a helicopter, because there's no landing strip.

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:16.560
You have to go by boat.

00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:24.360
It's a 60-minute voyage from Guernsey, and this boat is carrying supplies for the island.

00:20:23.600 --> 00:20:31.920
'Anything that's not grown or made on Sark has to be shipped in.

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:38.320
'It's tranquil here, 'because all motor vehicles apart from tractors are banned.'

00:20:37.720 --> 00:20:39.760
How strange is this?

00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:46.120
It's the first time I've ever walked along country lanes that have never seen a motorcar.

00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:54.320
And I'm on my way to meet the Nightingales, dairy farmers who supply the whole island with pasteurised milk and cream.

00:20:53.360 --> 00:20:59.400
'These Guernsey cows are the pride and joy of Chris Nightingale 'and his daughter Mary.

00:20:58.440 --> 00:21:06.960
'Until he started the herd 30-odd years ago, 'all pasteurised milk was imported.'

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:10.400
Is there a different taste, do you think, to Sark milk?

00:21:09.440 --> 00:21:14.400
Our biggest enemy at this time of year is the wild garlic.

00:21:13.680 --> 00:21:14.840
Yeah, I've seen it everywhere!

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:15.800
Yes.

00:21:15.400 --> 00:21:20.440
So you have to drive the cows like mad down the road so they don't eat that, cos if they get a mouthful of that it comes in the milk straight away.

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:21.640
So you've got garlic-flavoured milk.

00:21:20.720 --> 00:21:23.440
Which of course is the last thing you want!

00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:38.520
'There are no EU subsidies on Sark, and Chris rents his 50 acres of land 'on, would you believe, 'a year-by-year basis from seven different landlords.

00:21:37.560 --> 00:21:44.040
'Farming is in his blood, but he's got far less security than many 'of his counterparts on the mainland.'

00:21:43.400 --> 00:21:52.440
Everything you do as far as improving the land, re-seeding or anything like that, you're gambling that you're going to have it for another two or three years to get your money back.

00:21:52.280 --> 00:21:56.920
What would you say was the biggest challenge facing farming here?

00:21:56.080 --> 00:21:59.000
It's the sea.

00:21:58.080 --> 00:22:05.520
If we were to import things, they would have to travel mainland to Guernsey, Guernsey to Sark, and the costs of those two stretches of water are very expensive.

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:10.960
And is there a future, then, do you think, for farming on this island?

00:22:10.160 --> 00:22:15.040
Er, I worry about that, because it's good to have a balance of land use.

00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:22.440
It's the way things are, and we're just fortunate that we've got seven landlords who are willing to give us the opportunity to farm on the island, you know?

00:22:21.680 --> 00:22:30.640
So we're so proud now that there's no need for milk to be imported from Guernsey, the island's self-sufficient thanks to the loyalty of our customers.

00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:32.680
'And I'm off to meet some of those customers now.

00:22:32.200 --> 00:22:34.360
'This is a milk round with a difference.

00:22:33.440 --> 00:22:37.200
'With no vans, I'm using a horse and cart.'

00:22:38.480 --> 00:22:42.720
Julie, this is Sark's version of a taxi, is it?

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:43.400
Oh, yes, very posh!

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:46.680
It's either that, bicycle or walk, really.

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:50.400
And is there a strong sense of community here?

00:22:51.280 --> 00:22:52.520
Oh, yeah, there is.

00:22:51.560 --> 00:22:54.320
There's people doing things for people.

00:22:53.480 --> 00:22:57.320
We're raising charity money all the time.

00:22:57.000 --> 00:23:01.200
It sounds to be a very old-fashioned kind of community.

00:23:00.520 --> 00:23:08.080
Well, we're almost a step up from the Amish, except we've only got one husband!

00:23:07.400 --> 00:23:14.480
'First stop on my milk round is Helen Plummer, who runs a grocery store.'

00:23:15.800 --> 00:23:18.160
I have your milk delivery.

00:23:17.240 --> 00:23:19.080
Thank you very much indeed!

00:23:20.200 --> 00:23:28.720
'Much of Helen's produce is local, 'from vegetables grown in the field opposite 'to shellfish and meat.'

00:23:28.680 --> 00:23:31.800
It must be expensive, though, importing everything else.

00:23:31.160 --> 00:23:32.680
Yes, it is.

00:23:31.840 --> 00:23:40.000
You have to pay your shipping and you then have to pay your carter to come up the hill and deliver it to you.

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:42.760
I sense a great community spirit here.

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:47.280
If there's a wedding or anything joyous going on, we all celebrate and enjoy together.

00:23:46.600 --> 00:23:55.200
But if anybody is ill or passes away, we mourn together.

00:23:54.240 --> 00:23:55.640
And that is the way that the island goes.

00:23:56.160 --> 00:24:06.640
'Life on this tiny island seems to be about everyone pulling together, 'but this close-knit community does welcome newcomers, 'people like my last customer, Caragh Couldridge.

00:24:05.920 --> 00:24:12.840
'She moved here in 1989, and I'm delivering double cream 'to what the locals call her factory, Sark-style.'

00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:19.120
Well, it's only a little factory, but I love what you make here, Caragh.

00:24:18.200 --> 00:24:20.360
Chocolate!

00:24:19.600 --> 00:24:23.080
Why chocolate on Sark?

00:24:22.840 --> 00:24:34.760
When I first came to Sark, the first thing I noticed was how fantastic the cream was, and it seemed to me, "Why isn't somebody making fantastic "things with the cream?"

00:24:34.680 --> 00:24:38.520
So, here we've made some hazelnut truffles.

00:24:37.840 --> 00:24:41.480
The girls behind me, they do the rolling.

00:24:40.520 --> 00:24:44.240
They come across here, and then we temper the chocolate in here.

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:47.120
What it does is it will set very quickly, so it doesn't give you too long to sprinkle on your hazelnuts.

00:24:46.160 --> 00:24:47.400
I've got to sprinkle hazelnuts?

00:24:46.560 --> 00:24:47.800
Sprinkle on the hazelnuts.

00:24:49.880 --> 00:24:58.360
'Caragh started off selling her chocolates to visitors, but word 'quickly spread and now she has customers as far away as America.

00:24:57.600 --> 00:25:00.600
'So, just how good are her chocolates made with Sark cream?'

00:24:59.680 --> 00:25:00.960
Mm!

00:25:01.400 --> 00:25:02.680
Oh, lovely.

00:25:04.320 --> 00:25:07.120
Lovely!

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:11.480
I don't think I've ever been in a factory before which has such a fantastic view from its windows.

00:25:10.720 --> 00:25:11.920
Well, I know!

00:25:11.080 --> 00:25:13.000
We are very, very lucky.

00:25:12.160 --> 00:25:14.640
We get fantastic views from here.

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:16.160
It is so special.

00:25:15.520 --> 00:25:19.240
It's lovely to see the sea, and I think it keeps you sane.

00:25:22.680 --> 00:25:30.800
'As I leave Sark, I'm reminded of just how much life on this 'beautiful island is dictated by the water all around it.'

00:25:31.320 --> 00:25:40.080
Everything depends on the moods of the sea, and that creates an uncertainty that most of us just can't imagine.

00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:44.440
But what really struck me was the strength of the island community.

00:25:43.560 --> 00:25:51.000
They're all in it together, and it's that willingness to help each other that lies at the very heart of life on Sark.

00:25:53.920 --> 00:26:04.280
'Earlier, we heard how a weed 'called black-grass is sweeping across arable 'land and keeps growing even after being blasted with pesticides.

00:26:03.320 --> 00:26:07.560
'So, what else can we do to beat it?

00:26:11.120 --> 00:26:22.480
'Black-grass, a real menace, 'especially when it invades valuable wheat fields like this one.'

00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:23.840
If you don't get on top of it, then THIS will overtake THAT.

00:26:26.520 --> 00:26:33.640
'As we've heard, black-grass is becoming 'increasingly resistant to chemical treatment.

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:39.360
'For many farmers, 'it's impossible to kill with products currently on the market.

00:26:38.600 --> 00:26:45.040
'But we invented herbicides to kill weeds.'

00:26:44.200 --> 00:26:50.280
So all this begs the question, why don't we just invent some more?

00:26:49.600 --> 00:26:53.160
'That's a question for Jason Tatnell.

00:26:52.480 --> 00:26:59.000
'He's herbicide technical manager for the chemical company Syngenta.'

00:27:00.160 --> 00:27:01.240
We've got various research projects going on here, all screening different herbicides.

00:27:00.520 --> 00:27:06.720
'I've come to their research and development facility in Berkshire.

00:27:05.960 --> 00:27:11.480
'It's at the cutting edge of crop protection.'

00:27:12.280 --> 00:27:17.160
Jason, what herbicides are farmers using which, actually, black-grass has already got the better of?

00:27:16.240 --> 00:27:19.640
Well, I'd say broadly speaking there's about seven different classes of herbicides in use at the moment.

00:27:19.320 --> 00:27:24.760
I think the sad news is that black-grass is now resistant to all of those to a greater or lesser extent.

00:27:23.960 --> 00:27:25.240
All of them?

00:27:24.560 --> 00:27:25.880
Yes, all of them.

00:27:26.680 --> 00:27:31.880
We have been here before in the '70s, the '80s, the '90s.

00:27:31.040 --> 00:27:37.640
If something became resistant to the chemical, then people like you just invented a new one.

00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:50.280
OK, well, we are trying, but we need 100,000 compounds to find one potential herbicide lead, and then it's been taking up to ten years and £200 million to actually bring that to market.

00:27:49.520 --> 00:27:56.680
So does that mean because it's too complicated and too expensive you're abandoning British farmers to be overrun by black-grass?

00:27:55.880 --> 00:28:00.920
No, there's nobody here who would love to develop a new black-grass herbicide more than me, and we are looking.

00:28:00.560 --> 00:28:03.400
The reality is, as I say, the process can take up to ten years.

00:28:04.440 --> 00:28:09.760
'But Jason doesn't think it's a hopeless situation for farmers.

00:28:08.920 --> 00:28:14.080
'In fact, you may be surprised to hear the following bit of advice 'from a chemical company.'

00:28:14.120 --> 00:28:22.040
We would never advise the use of herbicides on their own, always as the pinnacle of a process involving other non-chemical means.

00:28:21.160 --> 00:28:26.200
Herbicides are just one strategy a farmer can employ to control weeds.

00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:33.480
The fact is, with black-grass we're in a situation now where no one element will win you the battle entirely.

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:36.440
Oh, come on, that's not what you've been saying.

00:28:35.520 --> 00:28:39.720
Actually, in the past, you said, "Throw the chemicals at the problem, it will all be all right."

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:52.680
Well, rather like with the talk in the press about antibiotics recently, if you rely heavily and solely on one element, you are going to be selecting for resistance problems much quicker.

00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:57.800
'Herbicide resistance means even chemical 'companies are promoting non-chemical methods.

00:28:57.240 --> 00:29:01.960
'In fact, there's a growing consensus that we should be using more 'traditional solutions.'

00:29:01.400 --> 00:29:02.560
Not entirely, though.

00:29:01.800 --> 00:29:09.240
There is another highly controversial debate around the edges of this issue - GM.

00:29:09.560 --> 00:29:16.800
'Glyphosate - among other things the main component of the weedkiller 'Roundup, is one chemical guaranteed to kill black-grass.

00:29:16.080 --> 00:29:24.760
'Trouble is, it would also kill the wheat, 'unless you developed genetically modified glyphosate-resistant wheat.'

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:31.120
Now, some see this as the obvious answer to our black-grass woes.

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:42.680
Others, though, aren't so keen, partly because glyphosate is in itself a fairly controversial chemical and also because that would open the door to genetically modified crops.

00:29:41.960 --> 00:29:48.760
'But farmers like Tom Bradshaw are looking for immediate solutions.

00:29:49.960 --> 00:29:57.720
'Back on his farm in Essex, Tom's turning to technology 'to hunt down the black-grass.

00:29:57.680 --> 00:30:05.240
'This drone uses special infrared cameras to identify 'patches of the weed.'

00:30:05.040 --> 00:30:22.120
We hope that by flying over the top of the field we'll get a map of where the black-grass is out in the field, and then in the future we're hoping we might even just be able to spray out the target areas where the bad black-grass is and then re-drill another crop into that area so that we don't have the whole crop loss that we've seen elsewhere.

00:30:21.680 --> 00:30:27.680
But killing off crops and starting again is a last resort.

00:30:28.280 --> 00:30:32.520
As the weather turns against us, I've brought a visitor to Tom's farm.

00:30:34.520 --> 00:30:42.120
Black grass expert Stephen Moss thinks turning back the clock is the best way to tackle the problem.

00:30:41.440 --> 00:30:47.440
But that involves rearranging the whole farming calendar.

00:30:46.960 --> 00:30:59.840
I think there is a need to return back to some of the old traditional methods of farming, even if drilling later in the autumn or spring cropping can help a lot because black-grass is very much associated with early autumn sowing.

00:30:58.920 --> 00:31:04.720
For Tom that's easier said than done.

00:31:03.840 --> 00:31:12.360
And it's not just moving his year around, Stephen also wants to see a return to more ploughing, which can bury the black-grass seeds and stop them growing.

00:31:11.880 --> 00:31:13.160
And then there's fallowing.

00:31:12.320 --> 00:31:18.080
What about the idea of actually leaving a field fallow, leaving it empty?

00:31:17.280 --> 00:31:19.120
Now, that really is a last resort.

00:31:18.320 --> 00:31:28.240
And I think that to convince us at the moment that we are going to be routinely fallowing part of the farm is a very extreme situation.

00:31:27.360 --> 00:31:31.320
Tom, you're fourth generation on this farm.

00:31:30.560 --> 00:31:34.800
How do you feel about going back to what your great-grandfather would be doing when he first got here?

00:31:34.440 --> 00:31:36.880
Our forefathers probably weren't that wrong.

00:31:36.120 --> 00:31:41.720
And maybe we've really got to go back to basics while using some of these modern techniques.

00:31:40.920 --> 00:31:43.560
We've got to accept we need to change.

00:31:46.440 --> 00:31:50.320
So there are some difficult choices ahead for farmers.

00:31:50.480 --> 00:32:02.000
But there does seem to be a growing consensus within the farming industry, even between groups which don't normally agree that chemical solutions alone no longer work.

00:32:01.160 --> 00:32:09.320
If we're going to beat black-grass and other superweeds, then maybe the farming industry needs to take a step backwards to move forwards.

00:32:21.240 --> 00:32:26.800
On the north coast of Jersey, the scenery is windswept.

00:32:26.600 --> 00:32:29.320
The terrain steep and jagged.

00:32:28.480 --> 00:32:36.920
A rare bird, the charismatic red-billed chough, once made the steep cliffs above these shores its home.

00:32:36.520 --> 00:32:42.880
But the call of the chough has been missing since the beginning of the last century.

00:32:42.200 --> 00:32:55.320
Now a special team here at Durrell has been breeding this enigmatic bird so that its call can be heard once more over the cliffs on the north coast of Jersey.

00:32:55.680 --> 00:33:00.800
She's very tame, she's very affectionate.

00:33:02.720 --> 00:33:07.960
Sometimes she's not too keen on men.

00:33:07.440 --> 00:33:09.280
Right.

00:33:08.440 --> 00:33:14.960
If she doesn't like you she might peck you or jump on your head.

00:33:16.320 --> 00:33:28.360
Just have a look at this mob here.

00:33:30.200 --> 00:33:35.720
The keepers at Durrell use a technique called double clutching.

00:33:35.240 --> 00:33:40.720
They remove eggs from the captive chough and hand-rear them.

00:33:40.040 --> 00:33:46.960
The mother then lays a second clutch of eggs, potentially doubling the number of chicks.

00:33:47.080 --> 00:33:50.840
She's absolutely beautiful, isn't she?

00:33:50.280 --> 00:33:51.920
Lovely.

00:33:54.160 --> 00:33:56.000
It's your favourite.

00:33:56.680 --> 00:33:59.360
And I just hold my hand out like this?

00:33:58.400 --> 00:33:59.640
Yeah.

00:34:04.240 --> 00:34:05.800
I think she likes you.

00:34:11.600 --> 00:34:16.120
When we tickle her like this, this is kind of acting like the male.

00:34:15.320 --> 00:34:18.760
So preening her and kind of stimulating her, really, and that's important with foster rearing.

00:34:19.880 --> 00:34:22.560
How far into this process is she?

00:34:22.199 --> 00:34:25.960
I mean, has she sat on eggs and reared some?

00:34:25.120 --> 00:34:26.280
Yeah.

00:34:25.320 --> 00:34:31.639
Before we acquired her, when she was in Italy she had built a nest before.

00:34:30.880 --> 00:34:34.520
When she came here she built the nest herself.

00:34:33.800 --> 00:34:41.120
We gave her a dummy egg, which helps stimulate her to lay an egg, and she incubated it really well.

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:49.320
Once we'd artificially incubated an egg from another pair, we hand-reared it for the first five days to ensure it was strong enough to be put underneath her.

00:34:48.520 --> 00:34:52.800
And pretty much straightaway she started feeding it herself.

00:34:51.920 --> 00:34:53.800
She's really surprised all of us.

00:34:54.159 --> 00:34:58.120
If I wanted to try and give her a little tickle, do you think she'd be up for it?

00:34:58.240 --> 00:35:00.600
I think she might be.

00:35:00.560 --> 00:35:06.120
Around her neck.

00:35:07.120 --> 00:35:08.760
It might help if I start tickling her and then you carry on, it might work.

00:35:11.800 --> 00:35:13.360
Good girl.

00:35:12.400 --> 00:35:13.680
Darling.

00:35:12.760 --> 00:35:14.720
Good girl.

00:35:13.760 --> 00:35:16.520
Oh, she likes me.

00:35:15.680 --> 00:35:17.400
Thank goodness.

00:35:16.880 --> 00:35:23.080
It's incredible to be this close and tickling a chough on the back of the neck.

00:35:22.560 --> 00:35:24.160
I never thought that would happen.

00:35:23.520 --> 00:35:30.200
When Jiana's chick is old enough it will be released on the spectacular north coast of the island.

00:35:32.040 --> 00:35:38.920
I'm heading to a project called Birds on the Edge, to catch a rare glimpse of these amazing creatures in the wild.

00:35:40.760 --> 00:35:45.640
And I'm just in time for tea.

00:35:44.680 --> 00:35:46.080
These are interesting looking feeding stations.

00:35:47.120 --> 00:35:49.080
What's the story behind these?

00:35:48.120 --> 00:35:49.440
Well, the idea is it's like a target.

00:35:51.120 --> 00:35:56.680
It really is like a dartboard, almost, that the birds can see from the air.

00:35:55.720 --> 00:35:57.640
They'll recognise it.

00:35:56.800 --> 00:35:58.200
Shall we get them in?

00:35:57.320 --> 00:35:58.480
Yeah.

00:35:57.920 --> 00:36:00.560
Brace yourselves at home.

00:35:59.960 --> 00:36:03.120
Turn your volume down.

00:36:03.080 --> 00:36:09.600
Right.

00:36:09.800 --> 00:36:12.800
Now, if we get back and they'll come in.

00:36:15.160 --> 00:36:22.080
With the decline of farmland on these cliffs, choughs died out in Jersey during the last century.

00:36:21.120 --> 00:36:26.880
Bracken took hold, stopping the birds finding insects in the grassland.

00:36:27.560 --> 00:36:31.320
It's amazing how they react to that whistle.

00:36:30.560 --> 00:36:32.560
Absolutely.

00:36:31.600 --> 00:36:39.360
We started actually training them before they were released so they knew what it was.

00:36:38.720 --> 00:36:41.160
Ideally what kind of numbers are you hoping to achieve?

00:36:40.600 --> 00:36:43.680
We could get 200-300, maybe.

00:36:43.240 --> 00:36:46.760
But 50-odd would be nice.

00:36:45.880 --> 00:36:48.280
But this process, it's exactly what Durrell was about.

00:36:49.320 --> 00:36:50.680
Absolutely.

00:36:49.760 --> 00:36:55.920
Having an endangered species like this and managing it to restore it back into the wild and bringing the habitat back...

00:36:55.480 --> 00:37:00.080
And working alongside the actual environment...

00:36:59.960 --> 00:37:01.160
And it fits everything else as well, yes.

00:37:00.200 --> 00:37:03.880
Eventually the choughs will be completely independent.

00:37:03.240 --> 00:37:07.840
But for now, it's encouraging to see them making a return to the skies above Jersey.

00:37:08.280 --> 00:37:15.600
Later on, I'm going to be finding out how these birds are getting a helping hand from some woolly friends.

00:37:19.160 --> 00:37:25.640
Now, what happens if you cross one of these... with one of these?

00:37:25.680 --> 00:37:32.240
Well, it's impossible, of course, but we think we've found the closest thing, so we sent Adam to meet them.

00:37:40.520 --> 00:37:48.280
Now, you'd be forgiven for getting confused about what's in here.

00:37:51.600 --> 00:37:56.160
The noise might give it away.

00:37:55.400 --> 00:38:02.680
But with its woolly coat, it looks like a sheep dressed as a pig.

00:38:01.840 --> 00:38:04.800
These pigs are Mangalitzas.

00:38:03.840 --> 00:38:10.200
They come from Hungary, where the winters can be harsh.

00:38:09.240 --> 00:38:15.080
They've adapted by growing thick, woolly coats to cope with the cold.

00:38:14.960 --> 00:38:20.400
So in this farm in North Chailey in East Sussex, it must seem positively balmy to them.

00:38:19.640 --> 00:38:27.560
And they're not only out in the fields, there's one in the farm cottage too.

00:38:27.360 --> 00:38:35.440
Most of Phil Beal's pigs are raised outside, but this one was the runt of the litter and needed special care, so he's ended up as a house pig.

00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:40.120
Welcome.

00:38:39.720 --> 00:38:42.720
This must be Alfie.

00:38:41.760 --> 00:38:42.920
Oh, my word.

00:38:43.920 --> 00:38:47.240
The same name as my son.

00:38:46.320 --> 00:38:47.880
Lovely.

00:38:47.800 --> 00:38:51.600
I can see the confusion now - is it a sheep, is it a pig?

00:38:50.880 --> 00:38:53.920
Well, now in the house, is it a dog?

00:38:53.040 --> 00:38:55.840
He's definitely a pig and he's going to stay a pig, Adam.

00:38:55.640 --> 00:38:58.880
What's he doing in here?

00:38:58.480 --> 00:39:05.880
Basically, a litter of seven and he was fine for a couple of days but then started going off feeding.

00:39:05.120 --> 00:39:10.440
And after about a week became quite listless and separated from the rest of his siblings.

00:39:11.480 --> 00:39:22.320
So I pulled him out, brought him home, warmed him up a bit, gave him a bit of milk... ..and 24 hours later he was walking around the house as if it was his.

00:39:23.200 --> 00:39:26.000
Is it a pig or is it a dog?

00:39:25.280 --> 00:39:27.440
Exactly.

00:39:28.800 --> 00:39:35.800
A dog looks up at you, a cat looks down at you and a pig looks you straight in the eye.

00:39:34.840 --> 00:39:36.240
Alfie, come on.

00:39:39.520 --> 00:39:41.680
Is he friends with the cat?

00:39:41.880 --> 00:39:44.040
Yeah, they're very good friends.

00:39:45.080 --> 00:39:46.480
No problem there at all.

00:39:45.520 --> 00:39:48.960
Just makes himself at home?

00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:49.200
Well, it kind of is his home.

00:39:48.240 --> 00:39:49.680
Is she going to beat him up?

00:39:49.000 --> 00:39:50.240
Eventually.

00:39:49.440 --> 00:39:52.480
Look, she's had a go at him now.

00:39:52.760 --> 00:39:54.560
Yeah, she will.

00:39:55.880 --> 00:39:57.160
Going to retreat back into the house?

00:39:56.320 --> 00:39:57.760
Yeah.

00:39:56.800 --> 00:39:58.320
Off he goes.

00:39:59.960 --> 00:40:02.400
What happens when he's a massive pig?

00:40:02.720 --> 00:40:06.360
He'll be reintegrated over the next few weeks.

00:40:05.920 --> 00:40:10.480
When his siblings get weaned from the mother, he'll go back.

00:40:10.360 --> 00:40:13.920
Tell me about the wool, the hair on them.

00:40:14.640 --> 00:40:18.720
They just produce this long, thick coat to keep warm.

00:40:18.040 --> 00:40:21.280
Is it on their bodies all year round?

00:40:20.560 --> 00:40:21.880
It can moult, just like any animal will moult.

00:40:21.160 --> 00:40:25.360
Some will moult more than others.

00:40:24.440 --> 00:40:29.320
If we have a hot spell it will thin right out.

00:40:28.520 --> 00:40:33.520
Can you use it for anything?

00:40:33.000 --> 00:40:35.480
I've heard of it being used in the past.

00:40:34.600 --> 00:40:40.320
It's not something that's desirable, but it's possible to spin, and if it's possible to spin you can make something with it.

00:40:40.760 --> 00:40:42.200
Let's go see the herd.

00:40:41.240 --> 00:40:42.600
OK, let's go.

00:40:41.680 --> 00:40:43.080
Alfie, come on.

00:40:46.560 --> 00:40:47.840
Come on, then.

00:40:58.840 --> 00:41:02.040
What happens with Alfie now?

00:41:01.520 --> 00:41:15.200
Now we've been introducing him more with his siblings cos he has to spend more and more time with them because in a week or so his siblings are going to be weaned off the mother and he's got to go back in with them.

00:41:18.720 --> 00:41:22.600
Yeah, bring a bit of food.

00:41:22.960 --> 00:41:28.360
So the ones that have been reared on the sow are much bigger than him, aren't they?

00:41:27.480 --> 00:41:32.320
Yeah, I mean, the size difference was apparent after three or four days.

00:41:31.480 --> 00:41:36.760
But it's just started in the last couple of weeks, just to slowly catch up, so hopefully over the next month or so.

00:41:36.720 --> 00:41:38.080
Just extraordinary looking pigs, aren't they?

00:41:37.120 --> 00:41:41.240
With all the hair and wool over their ears.

00:41:40.440 --> 00:41:41.800
Yeah, they're amazing animals.

00:41:40.840 --> 00:41:42.080
They really are.

00:41:41.320 --> 00:41:42.800
Incredible.

00:41:43.200 --> 00:41:45.440
And various colours here.

00:41:45.120 --> 00:41:48.960
Yeah, there's three main types.

00:41:48.120 --> 00:41:53.400
There's the swallow-bellied, which is a darkish black-back With a lighter underbelly.

00:41:52.880 --> 00:41:59.080
Then there's the red Mangalitza, which is pretty similar, but much redder than these ones here.

00:41:58.800 --> 00:42:04.880
And then when you get to the east of Hungary, you get the blonde Mangalitza, which is what we have here.

00:42:04.280 --> 00:42:05.640
How old is that sow?

00:42:04.720 --> 00:42:09.720
That sow's now about three and a half years old.

00:42:09.360 --> 00:42:10.480
They're a fair-sized pig when they're fully grown?

00:42:12.560 --> 00:42:15.760
What have you got here?

00:42:14.800 --> 00:42:20.000
We've got five ten-month-old Mangalitza girls here.

00:42:19.040 --> 00:42:26.440
And what I'd like to do is get them through to the nice grassy paddock over there to finish them off for the last month.

00:42:25.600 --> 00:42:26.880
Right, do you think they'll follow you?

00:42:26.160 --> 00:42:27.280
Let's see.

00:42:26.360 --> 00:42:28.000
They like a bit of apple.

00:42:31.000 --> 00:42:36.360
The Mangalitza isn't known just for its woolly coat.

00:42:35.600 --> 00:42:45.160
They're also famed for producing a meat with a high fat content, ideal for Phil's specialist range of salamis and cured hams.

00:42:44.680 --> 00:42:51.360
With a commercial pig, you're looking at fattening them up to about five or six months old.

00:42:50.680 --> 00:42:54.920
We take ours right up to a year at the moment.

00:42:54.080 --> 00:43:00.120
And in the future we'll look to take them on longer than that, to get the best out of them.

00:42:59.320 --> 00:43:03.520
Yeah, this for the last four, six weeks just to fatten them up.

00:43:02.720 --> 00:43:06.440
They can graze away and really add to that great creamy fat that they produce.

00:43:05.680 --> 00:43:11.480
Why do you want the fat cos most people wouldn't want too much fat on a pig, they want a lean pig?

00:43:10.640 --> 00:43:15.360
Well, that's the unique thing about the Mangalitza and the product that we make with it.

00:43:14.960 --> 00:43:21.440
And it's the air-drying process that the fat is really crucial to help it dry smoothly and slowly.

00:43:23.240 --> 00:43:28.200
We're off to Phil's kitchen so I can get a good look at this prized meat.

00:43:29.600 --> 00:43:31.080
It looks lovely.

00:43:30.480 --> 00:43:34.800
The thing that strikes me first off is really the amount of fat on that meat.

00:43:37.080 --> 00:43:38.320
It's quite deep, isn't it?

00:43:37.600 --> 00:43:45.520
Yeah, I mean, you've probably got 20mm of fat there, and with the Mangalitza the fat is so creamy.

00:43:44.760 --> 00:43:48.520
It's very high in monounsaturated fat.

00:43:47.840 --> 00:43:53.680
And there's intense marbling of the fat throughout the meat and this really lends it to dry evenly.

00:43:52.840 --> 00:43:56.160
How did you learn how to do the curing?

00:43:55.520 --> 00:43:59.200
Well, we lived in Spain for ten years, up in the villages.

00:43:58.400 --> 00:44:03.200
Come the autumn it's a ritual thing from each village to slaughter a pig.

00:44:02.800 --> 00:44:11.160
At first I was just helping a neighbour, then it became a hobby, then it became a bit of a passion and now it's turned into a full-time job.

00:44:10.320 --> 00:44:13.000
Wonderful.

00:44:13.840 --> 00:44:19.080
I'm getting a crash course in curing hams.

00:44:18.240 --> 00:44:21.640
No cooking required, just Phil's secret spices.

00:44:25.680 --> 00:44:28.480
Then make sure that we're rubbing in.

00:44:27.520 --> 00:44:28.680
Really rich.

00:44:32.760 --> 00:44:36.240
Now we've got our cure rubbed in, they need to go in a bag.

00:44:35.920 --> 00:44:43.160
They're going to be in the fridge for around three weeks, being turned every couple of days until the curing process is finished.

00:44:42.280 --> 00:44:46.400
And then it will come out of the fridge and it will be rinsed off.

00:44:47.040 --> 00:44:48.520
'And here's one fresh out of a fridge.

00:44:50.760 --> 00:44:57.360
'And just like a haggis, you squeeze it into an ox's stomach lining.'

00:44:56.400 --> 00:45:01.000
And then once you've got this all stringed up, it's into the dryer.

00:45:00.040 --> 00:45:01.440
Exactly.

00:45:07.120 --> 00:45:10.800
This is one of our drying rooms.

00:45:09.840 --> 00:45:14.280
What's so special about the environment in this room?

00:45:13.560 --> 00:45:18.680
We want this to dry slowly and evenly, not too quick.

00:45:17.720 --> 00:45:22.320
We're controlling the humidity and slowly bringing it down slightly drier.

00:45:21.360 --> 00:45:23.880
And the temperature, keeping it warm and just right.

00:45:24.240 --> 00:45:26.760
And this will stay in here for how long?

00:45:26.760 --> 00:45:31.040
This size will probably stay in here for about five months.

00:45:30.080 --> 00:45:31.200
Goodness me.

00:45:30.320 --> 00:45:32.720
So it is a slow process.

00:45:31.880 --> 00:45:34.880
It's a slow process, but one that's definitely one waiting for.

00:45:34.560 --> 00:45:40.080
See you in five months' time.

00:45:41.360 --> 00:45:44.120
Of course, we've no need to wait that long.

00:45:43.280 --> 00:45:47.720
Phil's got some laid out for us that's already cured.

00:45:50.080 --> 00:45:53.920
Here's some of our range and then this is the finished product.

00:45:55.000 --> 00:45:58.880
We're looking at something that's virtually 18 months in the making.

00:45:58.000 --> 00:46:00.400
18 months to two years in the making.

00:45:59.520 --> 00:46:04.960
Not something that you're going to put in your sandwiches every day, but a real treat.

00:46:05.480 --> 00:46:06.600
Oh, yeah.

00:46:05.760 --> 00:46:08.200
Good?

00:46:07.400 --> 00:46:10.200
That is really creamy, isn't it?

00:46:09.240 --> 00:46:10.320
Mm-hm.

00:46:09.400 --> 00:46:10.960
Lovely.

00:46:12.600 --> 00:46:18.680
I've got to say, Phil, that is some of the finest cured meat I've ever tasted.

00:46:17.880 --> 00:46:19.200
It's delicious.

00:46:19.720 --> 00:46:22.920
Obviously I have to quality test it.

00:46:22.000 --> 00:46:24.000
It's not a bad life.

00:46:23.120 --> 00:46:24.440
Mm.

00:46:23.840 --> 00:46:25.000
(Can I have another bit?)

00:46:24.880 --> 00:46:27.560
Of course you can.

00:46:33.040 --> 00:46:39.400
We're exploring the Channel Islands and I've travelled from Guernsey back to Jersey.

00:46:39.200 --> 00:46:44.400
Holiday makers have been flocking here for generations.

00:46:44.840 --> 00:46:51.320
Drawn by the gentle countryside and stunning coastal views.

00:46:52.040 --> 00:46:57.400
Once a Victorian hotel, Plemont became a holiday camp in the 1930s.

00:46:57.040 --> 00:47:02.040
Since then thousands of tourists have passed through its gates.

00:47:01.080 --> 00:47:07.120
But towards the end of the 1990s the attraction faded, the camp closed and was left abandoned.

00:47:08.200 --> 00:47:19.120
Thousands of people on Jersey wanted the land reclaimed for what they believed to be its rightful owners - the flora and fauna of the island.

00:47:18.320 --> 00:47:21.560
And what became a 14-year campaign got underway.

00:47:25.120 --> 00:47:32.640
Mike Stentiford is a former president of the National Trust on Jersey.

00:47:31.680 --> 00:47:35.960
He helped lead the fight.

00:47:35.040 --> 00:47:36.920
I'm meeting him on Plemont's adjacent headland, Les Landes.

00:47:37.440 --> 00:47:40.480
Tell me about the campaign for Plemont.

00:47:39.680 --> 00:47:44.320
It all started in the year 2000.

00:47:43.520 --> 00:47:52.240
This is when the holiday camp ceased to be a holiday camp and it was basically up for grabs.

00:47:51.360 --> 00:47:52.560
And the developers moved in, started to put in a few applications.

00:47:51.600 --> 00:47:56.200
One after the other, they were turned down.

00:47:57.680 --> 00:48:07.360
The turning point came when more than 7,000 people turned up on Jersey's largest beach to show their support.

00:48:06.600 --> 00:48:16.960
It was a huge, great passionate show of strength, that people really did feel very, very strongly about the coastline.

00:48:16.200 --> 00:48:19.520
The islanders were successful.

00:48:18.760 --> 00:48:26.160
£3.5 million was raised through donations and matched by the States of Jersey.

00:48:25.200 --> 00:48:30.640
Plemont was saved from the housing developers and the site was demolished last September.

00:48:31.720 --> 00:48:41.240
We need to keep these wonderful open spaces for body, for soul, for mind, for recreation, you name it, it is so, so special.

00:48:40.720 --> 00:48:43.320
Well, you've certainly enticed me to go and take a look over there.

00:48:49.520 --> 00:48:54.960
Now that the camp has gone, the real work is underway to get this site ready for its new residents.

00:48:54.120 --> 00:48:56.400
And there's quite a lot still to do.

00:48:57.880 --> 00:48:59.680
It's very barren, this.

00:48:58.720 --> 00:49:00.120
It is.

00:49:00.080 --> 00:49:01.240
It is at the moment.

00:49:00.320 --> 00:49:02.840
It's changed a lot in the last six months.

00:49:02.120 --> 00:49:03.880
Do you want to take a walk up there and have a look?

00:49:03.040 --> 00:49:04.240
Yes.

00:49:03.280 --> 00:49:05.200
Let's see how it was.

00:49:07.200 --> 00:49:12.120
John Parkes is lands manager for the National Trust for Jersey.

00:49:12.840 --> 00:49:16.760
It just looks so bizarre, like kind of a moonscape.

00:49:15.840 --> 00:49:17.840
It does.

00:49:17.240 --> 00:49:21.640
You can see why they placed it here, such a fabulous spot.

00:49:21.760 --> 00:49:24.680
It may seem like we've landed on a barren, sandy planet, but there's a lot going on.

00:49:23.720 --> 00:49:28.600
And in around 15 years this site will be as lush as its sister headland.

00:49:30.120 --> 00:49:34.280
We've looked at Les Landes as a sort of inspiration of what we'd like to see here.

00:49:33.320 --> 00:49:38.560
Dwarf shrub and heathland habitat is what we hope is going to establish here.

00:49:38.240 --> 00:49:45.960
When the camp was demolished, many protected species including rare green lizards had to be saved and were placed in temporary sites nearby.

00:49:45.120 --> 00:49:46.440
Oh, yeah.

00:49:49.960 --> 00:49:52.960
Slowworm.

00:49:52.280 --> 00:49:55.040
Protected species.

00:49:54.240 --> 00:49:55.720
That's right.

00:49:54.840 --> 00:49:59.080
And hopefully it will go and repopulate once everything's finished.

00:49:58.120 --> 00:49:59.440
Hope so.

00:49:58.760 --> 00:50:01.360
Is there anything I can do to help?

00:50:00.400 --> 00:50:01.640
There's a lot of work going on today.

00:50:02.240 --> 00:50:05.600
We're creating a few more ponds, so perhaps you can help us with one of those.

00:50:04.800 --> 00:50:06.800
Yeah, I'll give it a go.

00:50:14.720 --> 00:50:18.040
It seems like I've got a bit of help from some friendly firefighters.

00:50:18.720 --> 00:50:20.080
Oh, I'd love to have a go at this.

00:50:19.160 --> 00:50:20.320
Can I join in?

00:50:19.360 --> 00:50:20.720
Absolutely, yeah.

00:50:26.200 --> 00:50:28.600
Here we go.

00:50:27.960 --> 00:50:29.240
Whoo.

00:50:28.360 --> 00:50:29.600
That's got a force on it.

00:50:28.680 --> 00:50:30.440
This is like a live animal.

00:50:30.360 --> 00:50:33.480
And there's work to be done on the site's other pond too.

00:50:32.560 --> 00:50:33.920
Here we go.

00:50:37.840 --> 00:50:39.560
That's the last one.

00:50:38.600 --> 00:50:40.240
Yeah!

00:50:39.840 --> 00:50:41.800
Call that a tea break.

00:50:43.840 --> 00:50:48.440
My work done, there's one last person I've been told I have to meet.

00:50:47.760 --> 00:50:48.800
Nice to see you.

00:50:49.120 --> 00:50:51.480
Hello, nice to see you as well.

00:50:51.840 --> 00:50:57.160
'Stella Henley visited Plemont in 1949 when it was a holiday camp.'

00:50:57.760 --> 00:50:59.760
What was it like?

00:51:00.080 --> 00:51:03.040
It was a very basic holiday camp.

00:51:02.160 --> 00:51:04.120
More like Hi-de-Hi!

00:51:03.160 --> 00:51:09.120
You know, they said, "Good morning, campers", you know, like they did on the television.

00:51:08.840 --> 00:51:16.320
You know, they had activities such as archery, and they had games.

00:51:15.360 --> 00:51:18.880
There was dancing in the evening.

00:51:17.960 --> 00:51:22.200
How does it feel to see the site like this?

00:51:21.480 --> 00:51:23.480
It seems so strange.

00:51:22.600 --> 00:51:29.360
Of course, I was all behind them returning it to nature, and what a wonderful place it'll be.

00:51:29.520 --> 00:51:35.040
Then this place will become somebody else's holiday memories.

00:51:34.080 --> 00:51:38.000
It will, and I'm sure they'll find a wealth of beauty.

00:51:37.240 --> 00:51:38.440
It'll look lovely.

00:51:37.480 --> 00:51:40.760
It's great that wildlife will come back.

00:51:54.040 --> 00:51:58.280
We're exploring the Channel Islands.

00:51:57.720 --> 00:52:09.200
Whilst Ellie has been discovering how the site of an old holiday park is being turned into a haven for nature, just around the coast, I'm also learning about a habitat being improved for wildlife.

00:52:08.240 --> 00:52:13.840
The chough and other grassland birds once thrived here, but then the bracken took hold.

00:52:16.360 --> 00:52:21.280
During the summertime, these hillsides are choked with the stuff.

00:52:20.400 --> 00:52:31.640
It's like a blanket of bracken up here, so all of those species that rely on a grassland habitat to get their food simply cannot survive.

00:52:30.800 --> 00:52:34.680
The bracken destroys the biodiversity that makes this habitat so special.

00:52:37.280 --> 00:52:48.880
But the battle to banish the bracken has begun with the help of some Manx Loaghtan sheep, shipped over from their native home on the Isle of Man.

00:52:48.080 --> 00:52:52.320
'This fine flock is looked after by Aaron and his sheepdog Mist.'

00:52:52.800 --> 00:52:58.360
Why did you actually choose them as a breed to graze this area?

00:52:58.760 --> 00:53:06.760
They're essentially the Land Rover of the sheep world.

00:53:06.080 --> 00:53:07.800
They're a very old, traditional breed.

00:53:07.440 --> 00:53:18.720
What that means is they're good on the grass, they'll manage the grass, but they'll also manage the scrub, they'll manage the gorse, and even, what we're really trying to achieve here, is managing that bracken.

00:53:17.840 --> 00:53:19.080
How do they get rid of the bracken?

00:53:18.760 --> 00:53:23.560
A lot of people think they eat them, but they don't eat the bracken at all.

00:53:22.600 --> 00:53:29.880
What they do is they trample through the bracken backwards and forwards, using this mosaic of paths and networks that they use.

00:53:29.040 --> 00:53:37.880
That kills off the bracken in the path, it allows the light to get in deep under the big bracken beds and starts to get grass and other broad-leaf things to grow where the bracken was.

00:53:37.000 --> 00:53:43.040
And creates an area like where we're kneeling now, as opposed to just a big pile of bracken.

00:53:43.680 --> 00:53:46.880
It's this fleece, really, that gives them their name as well...

00:53:46.400 --> 00:53:53.440
Yeah, "Loaghtan" means "mouse brown" in the old Celtic term, so that's where the name Manx Loaghtan comes form.

00:53:52.640 --> 00:53:59.440
So, in fact, we've got here a special little product...

00:53:58.480 --> 00:53:59.640
Oh, look at that!

00:54:00.120 --> 00:54:03.800
..Made from one of our fleeces, for you to give off to Ellie when you meet her later on.

00:54:05.880 --> 00:54:07.760
I tell you what, it's an incredible...

00:54:06.960 --> 00:54:08.160
It's a good likeness, isn't it?

00:54:07.280 --> 00:54:11.080
Well, listen, thanks ever so much for showing off your fleece.

00:54:10.200 --> 00:54:13.160
You're welcome.

00:54:12.200 --> 00:54:14.800
You may now go back to all of your mates.

00:54:13.840 --> 00:54:15.080
Skippity-do.

00:54:17.400 --> 00:54:26.240
It's going to be a few years before the bracken is banished, but there are clear signs that life is returning to the clifftop grasslands.

00:54:26.040 --> 00:54:29.240
Ellie, how are you doing, all right?

00:54:28.520 --> 00:54:29.960
I'm good, you?

00:54:29.120 --> 00:54:30.600
How does this view compare to the ones that you've seen since you've been here?

00:54:30.200 --> 00:54:31.400
It's as good as the views I've seen.

00:54:31.680 --> 00:54:34.160
It's amazing, right?

00:54:34.240 --> 00:54:36.400
Yeah.

00:54:36.200 --> 00:54:39.160
Have you noticed how the acrobatic sheep graze all the way down?

00:54:38.400 --> 00:54:39.680
All the way down the cliff edges.

00:54:38.960 --> 00:54:40.320
It's steep.

00:54:39.400 --> 00:54:40.640
Very impressive.

00:54:41.760 --> 00:54:43.000
Oh!

00:54:42.040 --> 00:54:44.160
What's this?!

00:54:43.800 --> 00:54:45.520
Woollen gifts from you.

00:54:45.560 --> 00:54:47.640
This is becoming a habit, you know.

00:54:46.800 --> 00:54:52.240
That's to go with the woollen jumper, the woollen hat and the woollen handbag that I gave you a few weeks ago.

00:54:51.800 --> 00:54:53.240
Well, thank you very much.

00:54:52.320 --> 00:54:55.400
Anyway, that's all we've got time for from Jersey.

00:54:54.640 --> 00:55:01.600
Next week, we're going to be in Somerset where I'll be following in the footsteps of a legendary caver.

00:55:00.920 --> 00:55:04.080
And I'll be testing my taste buds with the flavours of the sea.

00:55:11.040 --> 00:55:12.360
Let's have one last look.
